\f0\fs24 \cf0 # Coregistration Tool \'97 User Guide\
\
> **Who is this guide for?**  \
> This guide is written for scientists (chemists, biologists, histologists) who want to\
> align two microscopy images without writing code.  No programming knowledge is required\
> to use the graphical interface.\
\
---\
\
## What does coregistration do?\
\
When you image the same tissue section with two different microscopes \'97 or when two\
slides are cut from adjacent positions \'97 the resulting images will not line up\
perfectly.  **Coregistration** is the process of mathematically warping one image\
(the *moving* image) so that it matches the other (the *fixed* reference image).\
\
Think of it like pinning two transparent foils of the same tissue onto a light box\
and sliding / stretching one foil until the cell outlines overlap as well as possible.\
\
The tool works on **segmentation masks** (black-and-white outlines of cells or tissue\
regions), not on raw fluorescence images.  Once the masks are aligned, the same\
transformation is automatically applied to all your channel images (e.g. DAPI, GFP,\
Cy5 \'85) so that every channel ends up perfectly registered.\
\
---\
\
## Installation (one-time setup)\
\
> **Ask your IT support or a colleague** if you have never installed Python packages\
> before.  Once installed, you only need to open the GUI in the future.\
\
```bash\
# 1. Install dependencies\
pip install pyside6 simpleitk scipy matplotlib numpy Pillow\
\
# 2. Open the graphical interface\
python -m mexalign.gui_full\
```\
\
---\
\
## Step-by-step guide (using the GUI)\
\
### Step 1 \'97 Prepare your files\
\
Place all files for one experiment in a **single folder**:\
\
| File | What it is | Typical name |\
|---|---|---|\
| Fixed mask | Cell outlines of the **reference** (will not move) | `ICP_mask.png` |\
| Moving mask | Cell outlines of the image **to be aligned** | `F_mask.png` |\
| Quality reference | A signal channel of the reference tissue | `ICP_DNA.tiff` |\
| Channel images | Any additional images to be warped | `F_DAPI.tiff`, `F_GFP.tiff`, \'85 |\
\
> **What format are masks?**  \
> Masks are greyscale or label images where background = 0 and each cell/region has\
> a value > 0.  Both PNG and TIFF are supported.\
\
---\
\
### Step 2 \'97 Open the GUI\
\
```bash\
python -m mexalign.gui_full\
```\
\
The window is divided into two areas:\
\
- **Left panel** \'96 where you set your inputs and start the run  \
- **Right panel** \'96 the live preview that shows how the alignment is progressing\
\
---\
\
### Step 3 \'97 Fill in the settings\
\
| Field | What to enter |\
|---|---|\
| **Data folder** | Click *Browse\'85* and navigate to your experiment folder |\
| **Alignment method** | Leave as `mexalign` unless you specifically need rotation pre-search |\
| **Fixed mask file** | Filename of your reference mask (e.g. `ICP_mask.png`) |\
| **Moving mask file** | Filename of the mask to be aligned (e.g. `F_mask.png`) |\
| **Parameter overrides** | Leave **blank** \'97 defaults work well for most experiments |\
\
---\
\
### Step 4 \'97 Run the alignment\
\
Click **\uc0\u9654  Run Alignment**.\
\
The right panel will show a live preview:\
\
- **Grey** = fixed (reference) mask\
- **Coloured overlay** = moving mask, resampled into the reference space\
\
You will see the coloured mask gradually shift and deform until it matches the grey\
reference.  The **pipeline log** on the left updates in real time.\
\
> \uc0\u9888 \u65039  **Do not close the window** while the alignment is running.\
> Use the **\uc0\u9632  Stop** button if you need to interrupt it.\
\
---\
\
### Step 5 \'97 Check the results\
\
When finished, two quality scores appear at the top of the left panel.\
\
---\
\
#### What do Dice and IoU actually measure?\
\
Both scores compare two images pixel by pixel:\
\
- **A** = the fixed (reference) tissue segmentation mask (`ICP_mask.png`) \'97 the cell\
  outlines of the tissue you are aligning *to*.\
- **B** = the registered moving mask (`registered_mask.tif`) \'97 the cell outlines of\
  the moving tissue *after* being warped into the reference space.\
\
For every pixel in the image, the tool asks: *is this pixel inside a cell in both A and B?*\
\
```\
Overlapping pixels (A and B both foreground)\
\uc0\u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \
\
  Fixed mask A:       \uc0\u9608 \u9608 \u9608 \u9608 \u9608 \u9608 \u9608 \u9608 \u9617 \u9617 \u9617 \u9617 \u9617 \u9617 \
  Registered mask B:  \uc0\u9617 \u9617 \u9617 \u9617 \u9608 \u9608 \u9608 \u9608 \u9608 \u9608 \u9608 \u9608 \u9617 \u9617 \
                           \uc0\u9608 \u9608 \u9608 \u9608          \u8592  these pixels count as intersection\
```\
\
**Dice** (S\'f8rensen\'96Dice coefficient):\
\
```\
        2 \'d7 (pixels in BOTH A and B)\
Dice = \uc0\u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \
        (pixels in A) + (pixels in B)\
```\
\
**IoU** (Intersection over Union, also called Jaccard Index):\
\
```\
       pixels in BOTH A and B\
IoU = \uc0\u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \
       pixels in EITHER A or B\
```\
\
Both give a number between 0 and 1.  IoU is always a little lower than Dice for the\
same pair of masks \'97 they are equivalent measures, just scaled differently.\
\
> **Why not use the DNA/DAPI image as reference?**  \
> A fluorescence signal image (e.g. `ICP_DNA.tiff`) binarised at >0 covers ~90 % of\
> the image area because there is always some background fluorescence.  Any cell mask\
> \'97 even a randomly placed one \'97 would score ~0.70 against such a reference.  \
> Using the cell segmentation mask (`ICP_mask.png`) as reference gives a score that\
> is genuinely sensitive to alignment quality.\
\
---\
\
#### Score interpretation\
\
| Dice | IoU | Alignment quality |\
|---|---|---|\
| > 0.90 | > 0.82 | Excellent |\
| 0.80 \'96 0.90 | 0.67 \'96 0.82 | Good \'97 suitable for most analyses |\
| 0.65 \'96 0.80 | 0.48 \'96 0.67 | Acceptable \'97 check the visual overlay |\
| < 0.65 | < 0.48 | Poor \'97 review inputs and settings |\
\
*Example:* the Kidney dataset typically scores **Dice \uc0\u8776  0.79, IoU \u8776  0.66**\
(mask-vs-mask), which is in the "Good" range.\
\
