\
## Alignment methods explained (plain language)\
\
The tool runs the alignment in three stages, each one more precise than the last.\
\
### Stage 1 \'97 FFT pre-alignment (coarse translation)\
\
Uses a mathematical trick (Fast Fourier Transform) to quickly find how much to\
shift the moving image so that the overall shapes roughly overlap.  This is like\
sliding one foil over the other to get the rough position right.\
\
### Stage 2 \'97 SDM registration (signed distance maps)\
\
Refines the position and orientation by comparing the *distance profiles* of cell\
boundaries in both images.  This corrects small rotations and shears.\
\
### Stage 3 \'97 B-Spline registration (fine deformation)\
\
Applies a smooth, local warping to account for any remaining non-rigid differences\
(e.g. tissue shrinkage, slight deformation during sectioning).  This is the most\
computationally expensive step and takes the longest.\
\
---\
\
## Alignment method variants\
\
| Method | When to use it |\
|---|---|\
| `mexalign` | **Default** \'97 works for the vast majority of experiments |\
| `rotation` | Use when one image is clearly rotated relative to the other (e.g. slide mounted upside down) |\
| `gradient_turned` | Legacy method \'97 only use if `mexalign` consistently fails for your sample |\
\
---\
\
## Parameter reference (advanced users)\
\
Leave the *Parameter overrides* field blank unless you have a specific reason to\
change a value.  The defaults are optimised for typical tissue-to-tissue registration.\
\
### Input / output\
\
| Parameter | Default | Plain-language meaning |\
|---|---|---|\
| `fixed_pixel_size` | `1.0` | Physical size of one pixel in the **reference** image, in micrometres |\
| `moving_pixel_size` | `0.2738` | Physical size of one pixel in the **moving** image, in micrometres |\
| `output_dtype` | `uint8` | Bit depth of output images (`uint8` = 8-bit, `uint16` = 16-bit) |\
| `tiff_3d_mode` | `reject` | How to handle 3-D TIFF files.  `stack_as_channels` treats each Z-slice as a separate channel |\
\
### FFT pre-alignment\
\
| Parameter | Default | Plain-language meaning |\
|---|---|---|\
| `fft_spacing` | `1.0` | Grid spacing used during the coarse FFT search (\'b5m).  Larger = faster but less precise |\
| `fft_erosion_enabled` | `false` | Shrink cell edges before FFT to reduce noise |\
| `fft_erosion_radius` | `1` | How many pixels to shrink (only relevant when erosion is on) |\
| `crop_padding_px` | `100` | Extra pixels added around the region of interest to avoid edge effects |\
\
### Coarse SDM stage\
\
| Parameter | Default | Plain-language meaning |\
|---|---|---|\
| `coarse_config.iters` | `100` | Maximum number of optimisation steps |\
| `coarse_config.learning_rate` | `1.0` | How large each optimisation step is.  Reduce if the alignment overshoots |\
| `coarse_config.sampling_percentage` | `1.0` | Fraction of pixels used per step (1.0 = all pixels) |\
\
### Fine SDM stage\
\
| Parameter | Default | Plain-language meaning |\
|---|---|---|\
| `sdm_config.iters` | `800` | Maximum number of fine optimisation steps |\
| `sdm_config.learning_rate` | `0.5` | Step size for the fine stage |\
| `sdm_erosion_enabled` | `false` | Shrink touching cells apart before SDM (useful for densely packed cells) |\
| `sdm_erosion_radius` | `1` | How many pixels to shrink |\
\
### B-Spline fine deformation stage\
\
| Parameter | Default | Plain-language meaning |\
|---|---|---|\
| `bspline_config.iters` | `200` | Maximum optimisation steps for local warping |\
| `bspline_config.learning_rate` | `0.5` | Step size (only relevant when `bspline_optimizer=regular_step_gd`) |\
| `bspline_config.bspline_mesh` | `(4, 4)` | Number of control-point grid cells.  More cells = finer local deformation |\
| `bspline_metric` | `mattes` | How similarity is measured: `mattes` (mutual information) or `msq` (mean squares) |\
| `bspline_optimizer` | `lbfgs2` | Optimiser algorithm.  `lbfgs2` is usually faster; `regular_step_gd` is the classic option |\
| `bspline_deterministic_sampling` | `true` | Use all pixels each step (recommended for reproducibility) |\
| `bspline_mesh_scale_factors` | `[1, 2, 4]` | How the mesh is refined across pyramid levels |\
\
